==== s2 vow prover (Addendum A.5.1), deliberately weak ⍝
⍝ A vow is proven when its measure is a linear function of the values it sees, and every
way round the loop (or to a recursive call) leaves it at least 0 and at least 1 lower. The ⍝
⍝ prover runs the body over symbols: a value is a block [tag c a₀ … a_d−1] meaning
c + Σ aᵢ·xᵢ (tag 0), the trit of its sign (tag 1, from ≶), or unknown (tag 2). The ⍝
⍝ symbols are the loop's stack (or the function's inputs) when they are ℤ, then 8 for the
ℤ moments of the enclosing block that the loop borrows. ⋔ on a trit splits the run into ⍝
⍝ paths, each with the facts its face implies (a chain [next f] of affine f ≥ 0); anything
else the prover does not follow leaves unknowns of the right number, as the checker ⍝
⍝ recorded the depths (ᚡᚧᚮ). A fact proves a ≥ 0 when a − λ·f is a constant ≥ 0 for
some whole λ ≥ 1. A measure that provably does not fall on a path that can be taken is ⍝
⍝ perjury. Addenda state slots: 7 the stack symbols, 8 the status (0 proven so far, 1
unknown, 2 perjury), 9 the function's name (−1 for a ⥀), 10 the vow node, 11 the ⍝
⍝ measure on entry, 12 flags (1: some paths were not followed, 2: a path ends in ↯), 13 the
enclosing strand (for its moments' types). A path is [S strand F vague]: vague once it ⍝
⍝ went through something the prover does not follow, so it cannot convict a vow of perjury.

